- org-sourceware-bzip2: 548 assertions (JVM, 14s) / 269 (nbb), clj-kondo clean. The `bzip2`/`bunzip2` binaries in both directions: we decode all nine levels over ten shapes; `bunzip2 -c` returns our input; `bunzip2 -t` runs libbzip2's own integrity check over our stream; the ratio against `bzip2 -9` measures 0.96x-1.00x and is bounded at 1.1x; a real .tar.bz2 from org-ieee-tar + this repo is extracted by system `tar -xjf`; multi-block and concatenated streams both ways. The portable suite carries reference streams recorded by tools/record_fixtures.cljs, which round-trips each through bunzip2 before writing, and flips bit 6 of *every byte position* of a reference stream, requiring each result to be either detected by name or byte-identical to the original.
- Bugs caught: the forward BWT's rank packing needs every rank below n, and seeding it with raw byte values silently mis-sorts any block under 256 bytes with spread-out values — the block then fails its own CRC on the way back (the test case that caught it is kept by name). And `(int "a")` is 0 in ClojureScript, so a sweep written `(mapv int "text")` compresses a vector of zeros: it looked like a 46-byte miracle for 18 KB of text until the oracle decoded our stream and returned 18,000 zeros.
